Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Needle Crosses Zero on the Third Baque
- Story & Context: The debut begins at 104 BPM with gonguê and caixa alone. Three alfaias enter one by one until the full baque cycle is established. Camila starts an ondes tone below the tonic and draws a slow upward ribbon curve across four bars. She is instructed not to reach the fixed pitch until the third major alfaia response. When the electronic line crosses zero deviation, Joana strikes one dry prepared-cavaquinho fifth, confirming the arrival without changing harmony.

  The next cycle redraws the curve in reverse. Davi holds one muted bass pedal while Rafael changes only the final low-drum accent, making the same pitch trajectory feel structurally different. At the midpoint, the resonant Martenot speaker is activated for one high note, then immediately removed from the routing. The brief acoustic bloom feels enormous because every previous electronic phrase has been dry.

  In the final passage, Camila begins the original rising curve but lifts her finger from the ribbon before reaching the tonic. The alfaias finish the cycle without her, leaving the missing arrival as the track’s final harmonic event.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close view of an analog center-zero ammeter in a cream relay cabinet, its black needle crossing the midpoint while a hand wearing the ondes Martenot ring controller moves along a ribbon below; maroon alfaia skin and prepared-cavaquinho strings in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “third baque,” “cross zero,” “hold the curve,” and “do not arrive twice.”

Track 2: Copper Busbar Holds One Note for Sixteen Bars
- Story & Context: Davi opens the second track with one foam-muted bass note repeated every two bars while Lívia establishes an unchanging gonguê pattern. The alfaias build around that fixed pulse, but Camila is allowed only one nominal pitch for the first sixteen bars. Her variation comes entirely from approach: below-pitch glides, above-pitch falls, short touch blooms, and abrupt cuts. The composition demonstrates that continuous-pitch articulation can create motion even when the destination note never changes.

  Joana enters with prepared-cavaquinho dead notes before gradually revealing a suspended two-note voicing. Rafael removes one alfaia stroke every fourth cycle, producing increasingly visible vacancies around the ondes attacks. No chord progression occurs until after bar sixteen, when Joana shifts one note upward by a whole step and Camila responds with her widest ribbon glide on the record.

  The final thirty-two bars return to the original bass note. The percussion becomes more active while the electronic line becomes simpler, ending on a dry fixed keyboard pitch with no glide at all.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long oxidized copper busbar running through a dark relay room with sixteen chalk marks beneath it, ondes Martenot ribbon parallel to the metal, one muted bass string and a clipped cavaquinho string crossing the foreground. Spoken fragments limited to “one note,” “sixteen bars,” “approach differently,” and “the copper does not move.”

Track 3: Relay Card Blue Means Fall Without Landing
- Story & Context: The EP closer is built around four physical cue cards used in the band’s live relay walks. Blue instructs Camila to begin above the tonal center and descend without touching the written tonic. At 100 BPM, Rafael establishes the sparsest baque on the record, leaving large spaces between paired alfaia strokes. Camila traces the blue curve while Lívia’s gonguê remains mechanically constant.

  Joana plays only harmonics for the first half, creating fixed bright points that the electronic line repeatedly passes without settling. Davi remains silent until the second minute, then enters with a muted low fifth that clarifies the tonal center the ondes has been avoiding. The effect is less like a chord change than revealing the floor beneath a moving object.

  During the final section, the ensemble removes layers in reverse order: bass disappears, cavaquinho stops, caixa drops out, then two alfaias leave. One gonguê and one low alfaia remain under Camila’s last descending curve. She stops the glide a semitone above the tonic and releases the intensity key slowly until only the relay-room ambience remains.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Blue electrical relay card held above an ondes Martenot ribbon in a humid industrial gallery, descending hand-drawn line printed on the card but deliberately stopping before a black target dot, lone gonguê and alfaia visible in the distance. Spoken fragments limited to “blue card,” “fall without landing,” “show the floor,” and “stop above home.”

